 many children they had  in fact the differences in family size  meant that the opportunity for selection  to affect fertility  increased throughout the 1800s even  after the demographic transition there's  been an evolutionary change in the  timing of reproduction  but it's not toward later reproduction  it's tort earlier  the entire history of a population of  french canadians living on an island in  the st lawrence river was traced using  parish records  women who began having children earlier  in life tended to have more children  over their lifespan  and daughters of women who started their  families at a younger age  also tended to become mothers younger  natural selection led to a reduction in  the average age at first birth for women  during a period of more than 120 years  natural selection has been shown to  favor an earlier start to reproduction  in other populations too  the framingham heart study began in 1948  to track the health and well-being of  people in a suburb of boston  based on two generations of data women  who began having children at a younger  age had more children over their entire  lifespan  other factors that contributed to larger  family sizes in the framingham study  were experiencing menopause at a later  age and being slightly shorter heavier  and having lower cholesterol levels  if those trends continue then women 10  generations in the future would be  expected on average to be even shorter  and heavier have even lower cholesterol  levels and blood pressure and become  mothers at an even younger age and  experience menopause even later  population bottlenecks deep in our human  past gave us our current low levels of  human genetic diversity  but that is changing fast  our global population is approaching 8  billion with about 130 million babies  born worldwide each year as we saw  earlier in the course each baby has on  average about 60 new mutations in its  genes  these mutations contribute to the  diversity of the human gene pool and  represent the raw material for natural  selection to shape our evolutionary  future  in addition there's a trend toward older  fathers in many regions including the  united states that will increase human  genetic diversity more and more rapidly  that's because fathers of any age  contribute more mutations to their  children than mothers and older fathers  contribute more mutations to their kids  than younger fathers  in short the total supply of human  mutations the raw material for human  evolution are in the process of  exploding in number  meanwhile gene flow is increasing with  the rapid movement of people around the  globe  populations today that were once widely  separated now have many more  opportunities to exchange genes through  sex and reproduction  gene flow makes populations more similar  to one another so one consequence of the  high rates of gene flow in modern human  populations  is that some of the differences that  once existed between human populations  may start to disappear  for example  people of the future may increasingly  have similar skin hair and eye colors  but more importantly gene flow can  spread helpful mutations much faster to  other populations than would have  previously been possible  this could mean for example that a  mutation that provides natural  protection against a particular  infectious disease could spread in just  a few generations to the regions where  that disease is most common  of course organisms that cause disease  will continue to evolve too global  travel patterns are creating more  opportunities for infectious diseases to  quickly shift hosts  based on our understanding of virulence  we should expect that some infectious  diseases will evolve to make us sicker  increased virulence won't necessarily  decrease their odds of finding a new  host  just think about how easy it is to catch  a bug from someone sitting near you on  an airplane  if our diseases get worse  will we just keep on evolving new  defenses leading to an intensified  evolutionary arms race  perhaps but keep in mind that  microorganisms can evolve a lot faster  than we can  that's a sobering thought we may be  helping our diseases evolve against us  but remember that we also have  microorganisms on our side  our microbiome consists of millions of  bacteria and other microbes some of  which evolved along with us and can help  us to stay healthy  those microbes will keep on evolving too  and it's in our best interest to ensure  that the helpful ones continue to have a  home in and on our bodies  maintaining beneficial microorganisms  that can promote health is a good reason  to use antibiotics responsibly  but it would be even better to develop  more ways to eliminate only those  microorganisms that cause disease  without also harming the beneficial  members of our microbiome  so many human populations have undergone  a demographic transition leading to more  opportunities for selection to operate  on traits related to reproduction  what does this help us say about the  future of human sexual selection  traits related to reproduction are  becoming more relevant to our evolution  than traits related to survival  in other words to consider our future  evolution we need to think a lot more  about sex and reproduction  darwin already suggested that mate  choice can be a powerful mechanism of  evolution leading to traits like the  elaborate tales of peacocks that can't  be otherwise easily explained  humans are sexually dimorphic on average  men tend to be taller more muscular and  have more facial hair than women  that suggests that sexual selection has  operated on our species in the past  sexual selection is still operating  today but now sexual selection can be  separated from reproduction  both by the use of birth control to  prevent pregnancy and by technologies  that promote pregnancy without sex and  births that don't rely on the birth  canal  let's consider technologies that promote  pregnancy  thanks to techniques like in vitro  fertilization couples experiencing  infertility today have a better chance  of conceiving children  in the past infertility that ran in  families would be strongly affected by  natural selection since people with  infertility contribute fewer genes to  the next generation  in other words infertile families would  become less and less common  but assisted reproductive technologies  counteract natural and sexual selection  and could make infertility more common  in the 21st century over one percent of  u.s births involve in vitro  fertilization in the uk it's over two  percent and in scandinavian countries  it's even higher  if these rates continue to climb it  could make future generations more  dependent on assisted reproductive  technologies and might lead eventually  to complete dependence on such  technologies for reproduction  another reproductive technology that may  already be affecting our evolution is  cesarean sections in childbirth  although c-sections have been performed  since ancient times the fact that they  are now much safer has made some of them  routine in some regions  in the united states nearly one-third of  all births are by c-section and in  brazil it's one-half  throughout human history the size of our  heads has co-evolved with the size of  the birth canal  but as c-sections become more common the  birth canal is no longer as much of a  constraint on head size  if at some point all births are  performed by c-section that would allow  us to evolve larger heads and brains in  the future  having heads too big to fit through the  birth canal would also make us  completely dependent on c-sections  reinforcing the trend  how far might our dependence on  technology go  some futurists believe that humans will  eventually merge with our technologies  leading to the creation of trans humans  in fact futurists can point to some ways  this process has already begun  we can already attach artificial limbs  to help us move 