nt of permanent colonies  beyond earth  such colonies could be a way to make  sure our species survives long into the  future even if some disaster occurs on  earth  but there's an irony to colonizing space  to preserve our species  because leaving earth might trigger  rapid evolutionary changes  that's because people living in colonies  on mars or elsewhere in the galaxy would  be subject to even stronger forces of  natural selection than on earth due to  the extreme differences in physical  conditions  in the case of mars its gravity is about  38 percent of earth's gravity  humans living there would be at greater  risk for bone loss because bone becomes  more brittle when gravity is weaker  humans living on mars might evolve  thicker bones at birth to reduce the  risk of life-threatening fractures later  in life like during childbirth  another reason why selection would be  stronger on mars is radiation  the thin atmosphere and lack of a  protective magnetosphere means that more  radiation hits the surface of mars than  on earth  humans living on the surface of mars  would be exposed to almost 100 times the  amount of radiation received each year  by the average person on earth  and nearly five times the annual limit  permitted for u.s radiation workers  how might people on mars protect  themselves  physical barriers will help but any time  spent on the surface for example to tend  crops would mean significant radiation  exposure  skin pigmentation might evolve to  provide some amount of radiation  protection  on earth the pigment eumelanin in our  skin provides some protections against  ultraviolet radiation  other organisms on earth use additional  pigments like carotenoids  most animals get carotenoids for their  diet  carotenoids are what make carrots orange  but we've seen one case in which aphids  acquire genes to make carotenoids  themselves  acquiring the carotenoid genes from  fungi caused these normally green  insects to become orange  if humans on mars 