n the  two populations people on mars would  begin to diverge from people on earth  through the usual processes of mutation  genetic drift and natural and sexual  selection  in a few hundred generations perhaps as  little as six thousand years a new type  of human might emerge  if that happens i'd like to think that  darwin wouldn't be too surprised  he knew that birds and tortoises living  on different islands in the galapagos  can evolve into distinct species  aren't species living on different  planets in our solar system much the  same  it seems fitting that darwin's theory of  evolution not only tells us about our  past but may help us navigate the future  of life  after all since the modern synthesis in  the middle of the 20th century  evolution has become the central  framework that unites all other  subfields of biology  you might say that what darwin didn't  know is how much his ideas would become  a framework for ongoing curiosity about  all of life  is the underlying reason why research on  fruit flies or mice can have any direct  relevance to humans  our common evolutionary history means  that their genetics to an astonishing  degree is our genetics  evolution guides our efforts to rescue  endangered species  we know that bringing together separated  populations can limit bottleneck effects  of genetic drift and may help them to  avoid an extinction vortex  evolution is the reason we know that  bacteria evolve rapidly and why we're  learning to be more careful when  prescribing and using antibiotics  evolution is why we are taking far more  interest in the human microbiome  we've learned that some of our microbial  entourage have been our long-term  evolutionary partners and that they can  help to keep us healthy in the  ever-changing fight against harmful  microbes  in short as theodosius debzanski one of  the most influential evolutionary  biologists since darwin put it  succinctly in a 1973 essay  nothing in biology makes sense except in  the light of evolution  evolution tells us who we are  where we come from  and where we might be headed  when charles darwin's voyage on the  beagle was almost over  shortly after setting foot in south  america for the last time  he wrote his sister a letter that  revealed just how much he had changed  during his five-year journey  his early enthusiasm for travel was gone  as he declared  i loathe i abhor the sea and all ships  which sail on it  in fact he never sailed again  but in that same letter we also see that  something else had replaced his youthful  wanderlust  he wrote  a man who dares to waste one hour of  time has not discovered the value of  life  it may be that charles darwin's most  fundamental discovery was his methodical  unifying curiosity about the value of  life  all of life  darwin suspected that all of life is  related on a tree of life all the way  down to the genomes we are learning how  to edit  charles darwin's very last book  just a year before he died was all about  earthworms  what they could do  what they could sense and even how  intelligent they might be  he wondered for example if earthworms  could hear anything  so he had his grandson blow a whistle  a son played low notes on a bassoon and  his wife played the piano very loudly  it's funny and inspiring how darwin  never stopped trying to learn what he  did not know  and we can do the same  darwin's fundamental idea how all of  life is connected and evolves has an  intrinsic grandeur that can inform and  enrich us every day  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

